<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nan Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1282 CE – 1931 CE (approx.) · capital Nan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Thai principality in the Nan valley that kept its own rulers for six centuries under Sukhothai, Lan Na, Burma and Siam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nan was founded by Tai chiefs in the upper Nan valley; chronicles give 1282 as a founding date at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pua-district">Pua</a>, with the seat later moved to the present city of Nan in 1368. It was linked by kinship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> and had early ties to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers of Nan governed a mountainous territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> valleys, salt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bo-kluea-district">Bo Kluea</a> and forest trade. They were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in turn of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a> from 1449, Burmese kings from 1558, and Siam from the late 18th century. Its temples, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phumin">Wat Phumin</a> with its murals, reflect a distinct Tai Lue-influenced style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom</a> Nan kept its princes but lost autonomy through the provincial reforms of the 1890s. When the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-mahaphrom-surathada">Chao Mahaphrom Surathada</a>, died in 1931, the title was not filled and Nan became an ordinary province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nan-kingdom.jpg" alt="Nan Kingdom" width="320" height="286"><figcaption>Image: Ubs6u!d-onstory · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nan_kingdom_late.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1282 CE – 1931 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chao Anantaworaritthidet, Chao Mahaphrom Surathada</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Northern Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Thailand</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mueang Nan, Principality of Nan, Nanthaburi</dd></dl></aside></div>

